
# A view definition: the document itself, or a named fragment of it.
#
#   view page
#     view text/heading
#       bind text, read title
#
# Mints the stdlib `view-def`, which is the component AST's own definition node, so a document lowers through
# `compile/view-lower.ts` and every backend emits it as an ordinary function with no new machinery. That reuse
# is the whole reason the dialect targets the zone AST. See note/term/view/07-lowering.md.
#
# `view` is the head in BOTH roles and the role decides the authority: in the `code` role it DEFINES a component
# with nothing held back, here it defines only the document and places components someone else wrote. A document
# cannot define a component because its file is in the other role, which is the security model stated as one
# sentence. See note/term/view/06-mill.md.
#
# A description is a `#` comment, which is what a comment is in Term. `view-def` carries a name, its parameters
# and its body, with no field for prose.

load @term/mill/code/view/node/mine
  find view-node

load @term/mill/code/view/seed/mine
  find view-like

mine view-def
  mine term, term view
    site name
    mine list
      mine form, like view-take
        site take
    mine list
      mine form, like view-node
        site node

# `take slug, like text`: one parameter, carrying its type so a use can be checked against the definition.
mine view-take
  mine term, term take
    site name
    mine maybe
      mine form, like view-like
        site like
